+<p><b>Raw cards / Graded cards (November 4).</b> The first row of the tab chooses the cards: <b>Raw cards</b> is the tab as described here, and its <b>Forecasting</b> and <b>Planning</b> buttons are two pages of their own; <b>Graded cards</b> (PSA and CGC) has its own Forecasting, Recording and Planning. Each page has its own address, and the tab reopens on the one you used last.</p>
+<p><b>Graded cards &gt; Forecasting (November 4).</b> For buying slabs at a vendor show and selling them on eBay: every graded row of the PriceCharting tab in your price band is listed and assumed for sale. The odds are this tab's method run on PriceCharting's monthly graded lines (PSA 10, Grade 9.5 / 9 / 8 / 7, since 2021): a slab's rank is how far its grade's price sits below its own 12-month mean (ten groups), its Chance how often slabs of that group made money over the hold (1 to 6 months) in this kind of market, 2022 on. <b>Buy price</b> = standard price x (1 - % under) - the eBay Shipping Fee, the most to pay the vendor; at the default 14.1% under (eBay's fee) a slab whose price does not move nets $0, so the profit is the move and anything bought deeper under. Standard price, Buy price, Sold, Net and ROI show Card Ladder over PriceCharting; the Chance is PriceCharting's. The filter buttons are the PriceCharting tab's with this page's own settings. Known flaws, for the graded model to come: PriceCharting's Grade 7 - 9.5 lines blend every grader, and grades without a line of their own (CGC 10, half grades, 1 - 6) have no score. The buys are the light green rows; QTY is how many. The <b>+</b> (green when on) puts a card and grade on Recording's <b>Looking for</b> list.</p>
+<p><b>Graded cards &gt; Recording and Planning (November 4).</b> <b>Recording</b> is for the show: every slab you record is a row (the scanning comes later; until then 50 sample rows, 5 of them bought, and <b>Remove samples</b>), with Forecasting's columns plus <b>Rank</b>, a <b>% Under</b> box of its own (it starts at the plan pane's % under - a vendor may take less) and <b>Purchased</b>: <b>Yes</b> = you bought it at its buy price (the money comes off the capital, the slab goes to Planning), <b>No</b> = the vendor wanted more (the row greys and gets no money), neither = still open. The light green rows are what the capital left still buys, best chance first among the open rows on view. <b>Planning</b> is the slabs marked Yes, at what you paid and the % under you paid, with what the basket is expected to make over the hold; <b>Record plan</b> (in the plan pane) keeps it, with the date, the hold and the other settings, on Forecast History &gt; Graded cards, through the collector. The three graded pages share one plan pane - except the % under: Forecasting's own, Recording's starting % for new rows, none on Planning - and one set of filter buttons, search and filter chips, apart from the PriceCharting tab's and the raw side's. The recorded slabs are kept in this browser for now.</p>
 
